===Etymology===
{{PIE word|en|wek(ʷ)speros}}
From {{inh|en|enm|west}}, from {{inh|en|ang|west}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*westr}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*westrą}}.

Cognate with {{cog|sco|wast}}, {{cog|stq|Wääste}}, {{cog|fy|west}}, {{cog|nl|west}}, {{cog|de|West}}, {{cog|da|vest}}. Cognate also with {{cog|fro|west}}, {{cog|fr|ouest}}, {{cog|es|oeste}}, {{cog|pt|oeste}}, {{cog|ca|oest}}, {{cog|gl|oeste}}, {{cog|it|ovest}} (all ultimately borrowings of the English word). Compare also {{cog|la|vesper|t=evening}}, with which it is possibly cognate via {{der|en|ine-pro|-}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|wĕst}}, {{IPA|en|/wɛst/}}
* {{audio|en|En-uk-west.ogg|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-west.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛst|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q679}}The direction [[opposite]] to that of the earth's [[rotation]], specifically 270°.
#: {{alti|en|W|q=abbreviation}}
#: {{ux|en|We used to live in the '''west''' of the country.}}
#: {{ux|en|Portugal lies to the '''west''' of Spain.}}
# The western region or area; the inhabitants thereof. {{defdate|circa 1300}}
# {{lb|en|ecclesiastical}} In a church: the direction of the gallery, opposite to the altar, and opposite to the direction faced by the priest when celebrating [[ad orientem]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1997|author=John Haskell; John Callanan|title=Sydney Architecture|publisher=UNSW Press|isbn=9780868403915
|passage=In two respects, however, the cathedral [of St. Mary's in Sydney, Australia] differs from English traditions: it is oriented north-south, not east-west; and its main entry is from the south (liturgical '''west''') between the two towers, in the French manner.}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=2000
|author=Mark L. MacDonald
|title=The Chant of Life: Liturgical Studies Four
|publisher=Church Publishing, Inc.
|isbn=9780898692990
|page=98
|passage=The seating for honored persons (clergy) is at the liturgical '''west''', opposite the entrance and lectern.}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=2007
|author=Patrick Malloy
|title=Celebrating the Eucharist: A Practical Ceremonial Guide for Clergy and Other Liturgical Ministers
|publisher=Church Publishing, Inc.
|isbn=9780898698077
|page=155
|passage=In most worship spaces, this will put the thurifer and gospeller facing liturgical '''west''', book bearer facing liturgical east (or the book on the reading desk), and the torch bearers turned inward, facing the book.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|author=Paul Porwoll|title=Against All Odds: History of Saint Andrew's Parish Church, Charleston, 1706-2013|publisher=WestBow Press|isbn=9781490818177|page=365
|passage=Throughout the book I refer directionally to the altar and chancel of St. Andrew&#39;s as situated at ''ecclesiastical'' east (to avoid overcomplicating matters), not ''geographical'' or ''magnetic'' southeast. Thus, the altar is located at the east end of the church, and the gallery, at the '''west'''.}}

